 0   0.50-  1.83  In fourteen ninety-eight,
 1   2.18-  5.54  three small ships dropped anchor off the coast of Kerala.
 2   5.84-  8.53  Their crews had sailed for almost eleven months,
 3   8.86- 10.24  all the way around Africa,
 4  10.52- 12.08  for one thing: pepper.
 5  12.38- 13.98  They had just started a story
 6  14.33- 17.26  that would last four hundred and sixty-three years.
 7  19.26- 20.70  In fourteen fifty-three,
 8  21.08- 23.55  the Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople,
 9  23.97- 27.08  and the old roads to the East fell into other hands.
10  27.38- 30.36  Indian pepper, silk and cotton still reached Europe,
11  30.83- 32.57  but only through Arab middlemen,
12  32.89- 34.60  and every one of them took a cut.
13  34.91- 37.88  So little Portugal, on the edge of the Atlantic,
14  38.06- 39.13  made a bold bet:
15  39.53- 41.55  if you can't go through, go around.
16  41.85- 43.47  Prince Henry, the Navigator,
17  43.81- 46.36  pushed ship after ship down the coast of Africa,
18  46.80- 51.59  and in fourteen eighty-eight, Bartholomew Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope.
19  51.89- 54.67  The door to the Indian Ocean was open.
20  56.67- 60.06  Ten years later, Vasco da Gama walked through it.
21  60.36- 63.53  In East Africa, he picked up a Gujarati pilot
22  63.76- 65.38  who knew the monsoon winds,
23  65.79- 68.80  and in May fourteen ninety-eight, he reached Kalikut.
24  69.10- 71.58  Kalikut's Hindu king, the Zam-oh-rin,
25  71.92- 73.77  grew rich by welcoming traders,
26  74.12- 75.81  so he welcomed this one too.
27  76.11- 78.74  The Arab merchants were far less happy.
28  79.04- 82.91  For centuries, Indians, Arabs, Africans and Chinese
29  82.91- 85.61  had shared this ocean by unwritten rules:
30  86.06- 88.70  everyone traded, but nobody owned the sea.
31  89.00- 91.35  The Portuguese wanted to own it.
32  91.65- 95.22  Da Gama sailed home with a cargo worth a fortune,
33  95.69- 97.94  because pepper that cost little in Kerala
34  98.25-100.77  sold for many times as much in Europe.
35 102.77-105.08  The next fleet, under Pedro Cabral,
36 105.42-108.83  set up a trading post in Kalikut in fifteen hundred.
37 109.13-110.39  Locals attacked it,
38 110.69-112.53  and several Portuguese died.
39 112.83-113.90  Cabral struck back:
40 114.29-116.08  he burned Arab ships in the harbour
41 116.28-118.13  and turned his cannons on the city.
42 118.43-122.04  Then he sailed south, and made friends with Kalikut's rivals,
43 122.32-123.90  Cochin and Kanna-nore.
44 124.20-125.66  When da Gama returned,
45 126.06-129.65  and demanded that the Zam-oh-rin expel every Arab trader,
46 130.11-131.62  the Zam-oh-rin refused,
47 132.01-135.11  and the trading posts quietly grew walls and guns.
48 135.41-139.68  In fifteen-oh-three, the first Portuguese fort rose at Cochin.
